    "alert_id": "ALERT_001",
    "title": "Rising creatinine and declining eGFR on TDF",
    "message": "Guidelines require serum creatinine and eGFR monitoring every 12 months for patients on TDF. Creatinine has steadily risen from 0.9 (2020) to 1.4 mg/dL (2026), with eGFR now 52 mL/min/1.73m². TDF is a known cause of chronic kidney disease, acute kidney injury, and Fanconi syndrome. Urinalysis shows protein 1+, which may indicate TDF nephrotoxicity. Evidence against drug attribution: no costovertebral angle tenderness, stable weight, and no oedema. However, the progressive trend over 6 years strongly suggests TDF-related renal toxicity. Occasional ibuprofen use (2–3 times weekly) may compound renal risk.",
    "issue_type": "toxicity",
    "severity": "high",
    "recommended_action": "Evaluate for TDF-induced nephrotoxicity. Consider substituting TDF with ABC or alternative per guidelines. Avoid nephrotoxins including ibuprofen. Refer for renal assessment.",
